      "content": "Sure, let's break down these words with a little bit of a \"goblin twist\" to keep things interesting.\n\n1. talossani: The root here is \"talos,\" which means \"goblin\" in the goblin language. The suffix \"-sani\" indicates a plural form, so it's like saying \"goblin goblins.\" No vowel harmony or stem changes are happening here, just a straightforward pluralization.\n\n2. kotoissamme: The root is \"koto,\" which means \"goblin dance\" in goblin folklore. The suffix \"-issamme\" is a bit trickier; it's a possessive form, so it translates to \"our goblin dance.\" Vowel harmony is at play here, as the \"-issamme\" suffix harmonizes with the root's vowel sound. No stem changes, just a harmonious dance of vowels.\n\n3. metsittäin: The root is \"metsä,\" which means \"forest\" in goblin language. The suffix \"-ti\" indicates a plural form, so it's like saying \"forest forests.\" Vowel harmony is present here, as the \"-ti\" suffix harmonizes with the root's vowel sound. No stem changes, just a forest of vowels",
